# Racket PPA Update to 9.3 - Notes

## Starting State (2026-09-15)
- Current PPA version: 9.1+ppa1-1 (jammy, noble, questing), uploaded March 2026
- Available upstream: 9.2 (May 2026) and 9.3 (August 2026)
- Repository: https://github.com/racket/racket-ppa (moved from takikawa/racket-ppa)

## What was uploaded
9.3 only. A PPA publishes one version per series, so uploading 9.2 first
would have left it Superseded the moment 9.3 published — and uploading
both back to back would have got the 9.2 builds cancelled mid-flight.
9.2 is in the repository as its own commits (source import, packaging,
tag `upstream/9.2+ppa1`) and as a changelog entry, so the history and
the package changelog record it; only the upload was skipped.

## Ubuntu 26.04 forced a build-system change
The old `debian/rules` was cdbs-based and included
`/usr/share/cdbs/1/class/autotools.mk`. cdbs 0.4.182, which Ubuntu 26.04
(resolute) ships, deleted that file as unused:

    debian/rules:4: /usr/share/cdbs/1/class/autotools.mk: No such file or directory

jammy (cdbs 0.4.163) and noble (0.4.166) still have it, so this only
broke the newest target. Vendoring the deleted cdbs files into `debian/`
worked as a stopgap, but Debian had already migrated their racket
package off cdbs to dh, so the packaging now follows Debian instead.

## debian/ now tracks Debian
`debian/` was already a fork of Debian's racket packaging. Rather than
porting their work by hand, the commits where Debian changed `debian/`
are cherry-picked, 26 of them, covering everything from the fork point
(their "debian: move man pages to racket common") through
`8.16+dfsg1-3`. `update-ppa.sh` records the last one adopted in
`DEBIAN_SYNC_COMMIT` and picks up new ones on every update; see
UPDATING.md Step 3b.

Only `debian/` is taken. A merge was tried first and rejected: Debian's
branch carries their own, older copy of the Racket source tree, so
merging conflicts on hundreds of source files and drags 1601 commits of
Debian and upstream history into this repository. Their
`debian/changelog` is left behind too — it records their uploads, not
ours.

What came across from Debian:
- dh-based `debian/rules` (`dh $@ --builddir=build`)
- executable `debian/*.install` scripts, which put compiled `.zo`/`.dep`
  files in the architecture-dependent `racket` package instead of in
  `racket-common`
- `/etc/racket/config.rktd` moved from `racket-common` to `racket`, with
  `debian/racket-common.maintscript` handling the upgrade
- per-architecture configure flags for the pb Chez backend

Kept local, and deliberately kept small so that future picks stay cheap
(see "Adapt Debian packaging for the PPA"):
- `Maintainer`/`Vcs-*` pointing at the PPA
- Ubuntu package names where Debian's differ: `libjpeg-turbo8` rather
  than `libjpeg62-turbo` (which does not exist on 26.04), and
  `libfreetype-dev`/`libgl-dev` rather than the transitional names
- `drracket.1` under `share/pkgs/drracket-core-lib`, its 9.x location
- no `debian/patches`: Debian's two patches are an upstream cherry-pick
  and its revert, so the series is a no-op and neither applies to 9.x

## Bugs found in update-ppa.sh
- Its stale-file check deleted every tracked file that was absent from
  the upstream tarball, which meant NOTES, UPDATING.md and the script
  itself. It now compares the previous upstream import against the new
  one. The 9.1 run predated those files, which is why it went unnoticed.
- `debuild -- clean` passes `clean` to lintian, not to `debian/rules`,
  so the test build's artifacts survived into the source package build.
- The Build-Depends check skipped the first line of `Build-Depends:`.
- The tarball-exists preflight accepted the 302 that
  download.racket-lang.org returns for versions that do not exist.

`debian/source/local-options` was added so that dpkg-source stops
treating this repo's own files (NOTES, UPDATING.md, update-ppa.sh) as
unexpected upstream changes; the stale-file bug had been hiding that by
deleting them.

## Ubuntu releases targeted
- jammy (22.04 LTS) — standard support ends April 2027
- noble (24.04 LTS) — standard support ends April 2029
- resolute (26.04 LTS) — current stable release

questing (25.10) went end of life in July 2026 and was dropped. There is
no current non-LTS release: stonking (26.10) is still in development.

## Verification
Racket 9.3 was built from the source package in clean Ubuntu 26.04 and
22.04 containers — 26.04 being the build the cdbs packaging could not do
— and the resulting packages install and run:

    Welcome to Racket v9.3 [cs].

The package split from Debian works as intended: 6135 `.zo` files land
in the architecture-dependent `racket` package and none in
`racket-common`, which keeps the 5290 `.rkt` sources.
